    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<div class="key-takeaways"><h2>Key takeaways</h2><ul>
<li>NOAA lists 309 thunderstorm wind reports and 71 tornado reports in Bexar County, and most damaging wind comes from straight-line thunderstorm winds and downbursts.</li>
<li>On May 27, 1997, downburst winds estimated in excess of 100 mph struck southwest San Antonio near Kelly AFB.</li>
<li>Bexar County is outside the TWIA area, so wind coverage usually comes from your regular homeowners or commercial policy, often with a separate wind/hail deductible.</li>
<li>Common disputes involve wear and tear, matching shingles, and whether damage came from wind or something else.</li>
<li>Photos taken quickly, the storm record, and saved receipts for emergency repairs make wind damage insurance claims easier to support.</li>
</ul></div>

<p>Wind damage insurance claims in San Antonio rarely start with a hurricane. They start with a spring or summer thunderstorm that rolls in fast, flattens fences, peels back shingles, and drops a live oak limb onto a roof. Some of these storms produce winds stronger than a low-end hurricane, concentrated over a few neighborhoods for a few minutes.</p>

<p>This guide covers the kinds of wind that damage San Antonio properties, what the NOAA record shows, how wind claims are typically handled, and how to document damage so your claim is easier to evaluate. It is general information; your policy language controls.</p>

<h2>The kinds of wind that damage San Antonio properties</h2>

<h3>Straight-line thunderstorm winds</h3>

<p>Most wind damage in Bexar County comes from straight-line winds pushed out ahead of or beneath strong thunderstorms. NOAA's Storm Events Database lists 309 thunderstorm wind reports for the county. Many involve gusts of 60 to 80 mph that bring down trees, power lines, fences, carports, and signs.</p>

<h3>Downbursts and microbursts</h3>

<p>A downburst is a column of rain-cooled air that plunges out of a thunderstorm and spreads across the ground. A small, intense one is often called a microburst. Downbursts can produce winds comparable to a weak tornado, but the damage is usually blown outward in one general direction rather than twisted.</p>

<h3>Tornadoes</h3>

<p>NOAA lists 71 tornado reports in Bexar County since 1953. Most were weak (F0/F1 or EF0/EF1), but weak tornadoes still tear off roofing, collapse carports, and throw debris. On March 19, 2002, six tornadoes touched down near Von Ormy and Somerset, the strongest rated F2. On February 19, 2017, a tornado that formed near the San Pedro golf course strengthened to EF2 as it moved northeast through north-central San Antonio.</p>

<h2>Notable wind events in the NOAA record</h2>

