<p>The 1997 downburst remains the benchmark. NOAA's narrative describes widespread damage across the southwest part of the city and more than 100,000 customers losing power. Many of the other events on this list touched specific neighborhoods: <a href="/service-areas/alamo-heights/">Alamo Heights</a>, <a href="/service-areas/helotes/">Helotes</a>, the northeast side near Judson and Stahl roads, and downtown. Wind risk is not limited to one part of town.</p>

<h2>What wind does to San Antonio buildings</h2>

<figure><img src="/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/Property-Damage-Adjuster-1024x683.webp" alt="Storm-damaged two-story house with a tarp on the roof, with a hard hat and clipboard on a table in front" width="1024" height="683" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Tarping a damaged roof quickly helps prevent further water damage. Keep the receipts.</figcaption></figure>

<h3>Composition shingle roofs</h3>

<p>Wind lifts shingle tabs, breaks the adhesive seal strips, creases shingles, and tears them off entirely, often starting at edges, ridges, and rakes. A creased shingle may lie back down and look fine from the street, so close inspection matters. Lifted or unsealed shingles can let water in during the next storm.</p>

<h3>Metal and low-slope roofs</h3>

<p>Standing-seam and exposed-fastener metal roofs can lose panels or trim when wind gets under an edge. On commercial flat roofs, wind can peel back membrane at perimeters and corners and damage rooftop equipment curbs and flashing.</p>

<h3>Trees, fences, and other structures</h3>

<p>San Antonio's mature live oaks and pecans are a big part of the local wind damage picture. Falling limbs puncture roofs and crush carports, fences, and vehicles. Fences and detached structures usually fall under "other structures" coverage, which has its own limit.</p>

<h3>Water that follows wind</h3>

<p>Rain driven through a wind-created opening is often covered as part of the wind loss. Water that seeps in through worn materials without a storm-created opening may be treated differently. Interior ceilings, insulation, drywall, and contents should all be documented if water came in.</p>

<h2>How wind claims are handled</h2>

<h3>Your wind/hail deductible</h3>

<p>Many Texas policies carry a separate wind/hail deductible, often a percentage of the Coverage A (dwelling) limit. On a home insured for $300,000, a 2% deductible would be $6,000, for example. Bexar County is not in the TWIA area, so San Antonio homes typically get wind coverage from their regular insurer, or through the Texas FAIR Plan where applicable. Check your declarations page now, before a storm.</p>

<h3>Common disputes</h3>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Insurer position</th><th>What can help</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Damage is wear and tear or age-related</td><td>Photos showing fresh tears, creases, and debris; the storm date and wind record; consistent damage pattern on windward slopes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Only a few shingles need repair</td><td>Documentation of creased and unsealed shingles beyond the missing ones; repairability and matching issues with discontinued shingles</td></tr>
<tr><td>Damage predates the storm or the policy</td><td>Dated pre-storm photos, inspection reports, roofing invoices, and prompt reporting</td></tr>
<tr><td>Interior water damage is from a long-term leak</td><td>Timeline of when stains appeared, photos of the storm opening, moisture readings taken soon after the storm</td></tr>
<tr><td>Code-required items are not covered</td><td>Review of ordinance or law coverage and local permit requirements</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h3>Wind and hail on the same storm date</h3>

<p>Spring storms in San Antonio often bring wind and hail together. The May 6, 2001 storm, for example, combined hail up to 4 inches with winds estimated over 60 mph. When both hit, the damage can overlap: wind-driven hail bruises the slopes facing the storm while gusts lift and crease shingles elsewhere. Because most Texas policies apply one wind/hail deductible to that storm, the cause usually matters less for the deductible than for the scope. It is still worth documenting both kinds of damage separately, so the estimate captures everything the storm did.</p>

<h3>Commercial properties</h3>

<p>Commercial buildings face their own wind issues. Flat and low-slope roofs can lose membrane at corners and edges, rooftop HVAC units can shift on their curbs, and signs, awnings, and canopies are often the first things to fail. NOAA's records for January 10, 2020 include an awning collapse at a business in southeastern San Antonio and roof damage at a manufacturing plant on the south side. Commercial policies vary widely in deductibles, coinsurance, and business income coverage, so review the full policy rather than relying on the summary.</p>

