<h3>Prompt payment timelines</h3>

<p>Texas Insurance Code Chapter 542 sets deadlines for insurers. Generally, the insurer has 15 days after notice of a claim to acknowledge it, begin its investigation, and request information. It then has 15 business days after receiving everything it requested to accept or reject the claim, or it may notify you that it needs more time, up to 45 additional days. Payment is generally due within 5 business days after the insurer notifies you it will pay. These deadlines are extended by 15 days after a weather-related catastrophe declared by the commissioner. Keep a log of every communication so you can track these dates.</p>

<h2>How to document a wind damage claim</h2>

<ol>
<li><strong>Stay safe.</strong> Stay away from downed power lines and report them to CPS Energy. Do not climb onto a damaged roof.</li>
<li><strong>Photograph everything before cleanup:</strong> roof slopes from the ground, debris in the yard, fallen trees and where they landed, fences, gutters, windows, and interior ceilings.</li>
<li><strong>Prevent further damage.</strong> Tarp roof openings and board windows. Most policies require reasonable steps to protect the property. Keep every receipt.</li>
<li><strong>Save the weather record.</strong> Look up the date in our <a href="/storm-lookup/">storm lookup</a> or <a href="/weather-events/">recent weather events</a> page.</li>
<li><strong>Report the claim promptly</strong> and write down the claim number, adjuster name, and dates.</li>
<li><strong>Keep damaged materials</strong> that are safe to keep, such as torn shingles or a section of damaged fence, until the adjuster has seen them.</li>
<li><strong>Track additional living expenses</strong> if the home is not livable.</li>
</ol>

<p>Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> covers the full list.</p>

<h2>Repairs, permits, and matching</h2>

<p>Inside the city limits, re-roofing and most structural repairs generally require a permit through the City of San Antonio Development Services Department. Cities such as <a href="/service-areas/leon-valley/">Leon Valley</a>, Alamo Heights, and Schertz run their own permit offices. Code requirements can add items to a repair that were not on the original roof, and whether insurance pays for them depends on ordinance or law coverage. Our article on <a href="/building-codes-insurance-repairs-san-antonio/">building codes and insurance repairs in San Antonio</a> explains how that works.</p>

<p>Matching is another frequent issue. If wind damages part of a roof and the existing shingle is discontinued, a patch may not match. How matching is handled depends on the policy wording and the facts, and it is worth raising early if replacement shingles are not available.</p>

<h2>When to get help with a wind claim</h2>

<p>Simple claims, such as a few shingles or a section of fence, are often resolved without help. Larger losses, disputed causes, or estimates that leave out obvious damage may benefit from a second look. San Antonio Public Adjuster, a service of [[company]], helps property owners with <a href="/services/wind-damage-claims/">wind damage claims</a> and broader <a href="/services/storm-damage-claims/">storm damage claims</a> throughout San Antonio and Bexar County. A public adjuster can inspect, document, prepare an estimate in Xactimate, and present the claim on your behalf. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement. Outcomes depend on your policy and the facts.</p>

<p>If the insurer's estimate seems low, our guide on <a href="/how-to-review-underpaid-roof-insurance-estimate/">how to review an underpaid roof insurance estimate</a> walks through it line by line. For legal questions, such as lawsuit deadlines or the pre-suit notice required under Texas Insurance Code Chapter 542A for weather-related claims, consult a Texas attorney.</p>

